  "journey":
    'The following is an illustrative experience to test with the owner. It describes a possible improved journey, not a claim about the present site or a completed implementation.\n\n### A homeowner considering landscape construction\n**Proposed entry:** "Planning an outdoor project? See the kind of work we do and tell us what you have in mind."\n\nBegin with the agreed priority services and service area. Show relevant completed work alongside plain-language explanations of the problem solved. A retaining-wall example should help a visitor judge whether the work resembles their own need; it should not imply engineering suitability or a price before review.\n\n**Next action:** "Tell us about your project." Ask only for information the team needs to assess an enquiry and explain what happens after it is received. Agree any response-time wording with the customer rather than inventing a service promise.\n\n### A shopper looking for garden decor\n**Proposed entry:** "Looking for fountains, statuary or garden decor? Visit Braemor Gardens."\n\nWhere the businesses link to one another, identify the retail destination explicitly. Use the customer-approved location and contact details, and confirm opening information before publishing. A shopper should not have to work out which business handles a retail question.\n\n**Next action:** A clearly labelled Braemor link or approved store contact route. It should not redirect a landscaping enquiry into an unrelated retail process.\n\n### A returning or referred customer\nKeep a direct contact option easy to find. Explain when to use the office route and when another route is appropriate, based on the customer\'s operating arrangements. Do not silently substitute a field number for an office number.\n\n### Acceptance test\nOn phone and desktop, a visitor can identify the business, recognize the relevant service or retail offer and reach the intended contact destination. The team can explain where an enquiry arrives and who handles it. Form submissions, delivery checks and account changes occur only with authorization.',
  "discovery":
    "### 1  Start with the owner's explanation\nConfirm the decision-maker, priority services, service area and the enquiries the business most wants. Ask when visibility seemed to change, which searches were used and whether website or business-location changes occurred around that time. Clarify the relationship between ACA and Braemor and how each should be presented.\n\n**Output:** An agreed problem statement, priority customer journeys and the facts needed to approve scope. Kelly's Customer Care role does not by itself establish authority to approve changes.\n\n### 2  Review authorized search evidence\nReuse the public checks already completed. With permission, inspect available Search Console history and relevant Business Profile information. Separate website search clicks and impressions from Maps interactions. Record gaps in available history and distinguish hypotheses from conclusions.\n\n**Output:** A dated baseline and a short list of evidence-supported causes or questions. If access is unavailable, agree a narrower public review before work begins; do not substitute guessed analytics.\n\n### 3  Complete local and social checks\nConfirm the two Google listings with the owner, including category, address or service area, phones and hours. Review an agreed accessible sample of feedback with dates and attribution. Inspect the supplied ACA and Braemor Facebook destinations directly; failure to find a page in search is not evidence that it is absent. Instagram remains access-limited and needs owner-supplied or authorized verification. [4-7]\n\n**Output:** A separate record for each brand, a supplied-link disposition and a list of corrections requiring approval. Do not reuse unverified follower counts or treat Braemor's audience as ACA's.\n\n### 4  Test pages and compare a fair local sample\nAgree a bounded set of representative pages and customer routes. Check mobile presentation, navigation and enquiry handling, then investigate technical items only where evidence warrants it. After the service area and services are confirmed, compare up to three relevant local alternatives using the same searches and criteria.\n\n**Output:** A prioritized issue register and comparison explaining what customers can understand or do more easily elsewhere. This is a scoped sample, not an exhaustive market or technical audit.",
